Kevin Wayne <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> writes:
[two pronunciations of <th>]
> Anglo Saxon (Old English) had two extra letters to denote these two
> sounds: (iirc) "thorn" for the unvocalized, "eth" for the vocalized.
> In HTML, &THORN;, &thorn;, &ETH;, and &eth; will produce the capital
> and lowercase characters of each of these letters, respectively.
> Apparently they're still used in Icelandic.

You don't remember correctly; Old English used either thorn or eth for
those "th" sounds with no special preference.  That was because the
"vocalized", correctly called _voiced_, fricative occurred only between
other voiced sounds, and the "unvocalized", correctly _unvoiced_,
occurred otherwise.  So there were no words beginning with the voiced
"th" sound in Old English.

In phonetic transcription, though, the "eth" character means only the
voiced "th" sound; the unvoiced "th" is represented with the Greek
letter theta.  

> These letters were eventually replaced by the 'th' blend we have now.
> I suppose the reasoning would be that pronouncing a "t" with a hard
> aspiration behind it ("h") gives some approximation to the unvocalized
> 'th'. Sometimes, in transliterating other languages into English, 'dh'
> will be used for the vocalized 'th' sound; that seems to make as much
> sense, for the same reason.

I don't know of any reasoning for the "th" digraph.  Earliest OE texts,
before 900, are said to have used it but it was replaced by thorn/eth.
During the Middle English period, it was begun to be used again and it
was standardized in early Modern English period by printers.  "Dh" for
the voiced fricative is logical since "d" is a voiced "t". :>

